/* Neurosciences, Neuroanatomy, neurophysiology and neurochemistry. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).neuro = (window.PA_CARDS.neuro || []).concat([
/* ---------- Cortex and localisation ---------- */
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","On which gyrus does the primary motor cortex lie?","The precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)","It sits just in front of the central sulcus in the frontal lobe. Each side controls voluntary movement of the opposite side of the body."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","Which part of the cortex is the last to mature?","The prefrontal cortex","Myelination and synaptic pruning here continue into the mid-20s. This fits the poorer impulse control and planning seen in adolescence."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","Roughly what share of the human cerebral cortex is prefrontal cortex?","About a third (around 30%)","The prefrontal cortex is much larger in humans than in other primates. It supports planning, judgement and social behaviour."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","Which prefrontal region is most linked to executive functions such as planning and set shifting?","The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex","It holds goals in working memory and switches strategy when rules change. Damage gives a 'dysexecutive' picture with perseveration."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","A 59-year-old man in the memory clinic picks up the doctor's pen and glasses and starts using them, even when asked not to. Damage to which lobe is likely?","The frontal lobe","Without frontal inhibition, objects in reach act as commands. The person picks up and uses them even when told not to."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","Which frontal region is most linked to judging the social value of actions and people?","The orbitofrontal cortex","It weighs reward and social cues to guide behaviour. Damage leaves intellect intact but social conduct poor."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","What personality picture follows orbitofrontal damage?","Disinhibited, impulsive, tactless behaviour ('pseudopsychopathic')","The orbitofrontal cortex normally brakes socially unacceptable impulses. Loss of this brake gives jocularity, disinhibition and poor judgement."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","What personality picture follows medial frontal and anterior cingulate damage?","Apathy and abulia ('pseudodepressed'), up to akinetic mutism","These areas drive motivation and initiation. Damage leaves the person passive, slow and lacking spontaneous action."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","A 62-year-old man in the memory clinic has new disinhibition, poor judgement, irritability and urinary incontinence. Which lobe is most likely affected?","The frontal lobe","The orbitofrontal cortex controls social behaviour. The medial frontal lobe holds a cortical centre for bladder control, so incontinence without concern is typical."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","In which region are mirror neurons mainly found in humans?","The inferior frontal cortex (with the inferior parietal lobule)","Mirror neurons fire both when we act and when we watch others act. They are thought to support imitation and empathy."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","Where is Broca's area?","The posterior inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere (Brodmann areas 44 and 45)","It plans the motor sequences of speech. Damage gives non-fluent, effortful speech with relatively good comprehension."],
["Cortex and localisation","Frontal lobe","A 58-year-old right-handed woman on the stroke ward has halting, effortful speech and a weak right arm, but understands questions well. What type of aphasia is this?","Broca's (expressive) aphasia","A left frontal lesion hits Broca's area and the nearby motor cortex. Hence non-fluent speech with right-sided weakness and preserved comprehension."],
["Cortex and localisation","Parietal lobe","On which gyrus does the primary somatosensory cortex lie?","The postcentral gyrus (Brodmann areas 3, 1 and 2)","It sits just behind the central sulcus in the parietal lobe. It receives touch, vibration and joint position from the opposite side."],
["Cortex and localisation","Parietal lobe","A 67-year-old right-handed woman can no longer work out her change in shops after a stroke. Damage to which gyrus is most likely?","The angular gyrus of the dominant parietal lobe","It links number symbols to their meaning. Damage causes acalculia, often as part of Gerstmann syndrome."],
["Cortex and localisation","Parietal lobe","What are the four features of Gerstmann syndrome?","Acalculia, agraphia, finger agnosia and left-right disorientation","All four point to a lesion of the dominant (usually left) angular gyrus. Each relies on mapping symbols or body parts in space."],
["Cortex and localisation","Parietal lobe","A 64-year-old right-handed man after a left parietal stroke cannot name or point to his own fingers on request. What is this sign called?","Finger agnosia","It is part of Gerstmann syndrome from dominant parietal damage. Look also for acalculia, agraphia and left-right confusion."],
["Cortex and localisation","Parietal lobe","A 70-year-old right-handed man after a right parietal stroke cannot copy a simple drawing of a cube. What is this called?","Constructional apraxia","The non-dominant parietal lobe integrates spatial relations. Damage breaks the ability to assemble parts into a whole."],
["Cortex and localisation","Parietal lobe","A 76-year-old woman on an acute medical ward after a right hemisphere stroke insists her paralysed left arm works normally. What is this sign?","Anosognosia","Denial or unawareness of a deficit is typical of non-dominant parietal lesions. It often comes with left-sided neglect."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","Where is Wernicke's area?","The posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere (Brodmann area 22)","It decodes heard words into meaning. Damage gives fluent but empty speech with poor comprehension."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","A 66-year-old man on the stroke ward speaks fluently but uses made-up words and cannot follow commands. He seems unaware of the problem. Where is the lesion?","The dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke's area)","This is Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia. Lack of insight can be mistaken for psychosis or confusion."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","Where is the primary auditory cortex?","Heschl's gyrus (transverse temporal gyri, Brodmann areas 41 and 42)","It lies on the upper surface of the temporal lobe within the lateral sulcus. Each side gets input from both ears."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","A 50-year-old man has rapid personality change, memory problems and loss of the upper outer quarter of the visual field on one side. Where is the lesion?","The temporal lobe","Lower optic radiation fibres loop forward through the temporal lobe (Meyer's loop). Damage there gives a contralateral superior quadrantanopia."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","Which gyrus is most linked to face recognition?","The fusiform gyrus (occipitotemporal)","Damage, usually bilateral or right-sided, causes prosopagnosia. The person can see the face but not tell whose it is."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","Which lobe gives rise to most seizures with an aura?","The temporal lobe","The aura is the start of a focal seizure. Medial temporal onset often gives smells, deja vu, fear or a rising stomach feeling."],
["Cortex and localisation","Temporal and occipital lobes","A 28-year-old woman at the epilepsy clinic gets brief smells of burning rubber before losing awareness. Where do these seizures most likely start?","The uncus (medial temporal lobe)","The uncus holds primary olfactory cortex. Smell auras from here are called uncinate fits."],
["Cortex and localisation","Sulci and landmarks","What separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes?","The lateral (Sylvian) sulcus","It is the deep fissure on the side of each hemisphere. The insula lies hidden in its depths."],
["Cortex and localisation","Cortical cells","What is the most common type of neuron in the cerebral cortex?","The pyramidal cell (about two-thirds to three-quarters of cortical neurons)","They are glutamatergic projection neurons with a triangular cell body. Their long axons carry cortical output."],
["Cortex and localisation","Cortical cells","Which cells dominate cortical layer IV, the layer that receives thalamic input?","Stellate (granule) cells","Layer IV is the main input layer. It is thick in sensory cortex and thin in motor cortex."],
["Cortex and localisation","Cortical cells","What are the largest neurons in the human nervous system?","Betz cells","They are giant pyramidal cells in layer V of the primary motor cortex. Their axons run in the corticospinal tract."],
["Cortex and localisation","Dominance","Roughly what proportion of right-handed people are left-hemisphere dominant for language?","Almost all (95 to 99%)","Language and handedness are usually controlled by the same hemisphere. Right-sided language dominance in a right-hander is rare."],
["Cortex and localisation","Dominance","Roughly what proportion of left-handed people are left-hemisphere dominant for language?","About two-thirds (around 67 to 70%)","So most left-handers still have left-sided language. The rest have right-sided or mixed dominance."],
["Cortex and localisation","Dominance","Which type of memory relies more on the non-dominant hemisphere?","Pictorial (non-verbal, visual) memory","Verbal memory is mainly left-sided, and picture memory mainly right-sided. People may blur this by naming pictures to themselves."],
["Cortex and localisation","Aphasias","Which language problem follows a lesion of the dominant angular gyrus?","Alexia with agraphia","The angular gyrus links written symbols to word meaning. Without it the person can neither read nor write."],
["Cortex and localisation","Aphasias","Where is nominal (anomic) aphasia classically localised?","The dominant temporoparietal region, especially the angular gyrus","Word-finding is the main problem while fluency and comprehension are kept. It is also common in diffuse brain disease, so it localises poorly."],
["Cortex and localisation","Aphasias","Damage to which tract causes conduction aphasia?","The arcuate fasciculus","It links Wernicke's and Broca's areas. Speech is fluent and understood, but repetition is badly impaired."],
["Cortex and localisation","Aphasias","Which vascular territory supplies Broca's and Wernicke's areas?","The middle cerebral artery (carotid circulation)","This is why a carotid transient ischaemic attack can cause brief aphasia. Vertebrobasilar ischaemia gives dysarthria, not aphasia."],
["Cortex and localisation","Aphasias","A 71-year-old right-handed man has 20 minutes of being unable to find or understand words, then fully recovers. Which arterial system was involved?","The left carotid (anterior) circulation","Language areas are fed by the middle cerebral artery, a carotid branch. A transient aphasia is a carotid TIA."],
/* ---------- Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum ---------- */
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Papez circuit","Name the structures of the Papez circuit in order.","Hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamic nucleus, cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, back to hippocampus","The mammillothalamic tract links the mammillary bodies to the anterior thalamus. The loop is now linked more with memory than with emotion."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Papez circuit","Is Heschl's gyrus part of the Papez circuit?","No","Heschl's gyrus is primary auditory cortex. Papez structures are hippocampal, mammillary, thalamic and cingulate."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hippocampus","In which structure is the dentate gyrus found?","The hippocampal formation","The dentate gyrus is the main input stage of the hippocampus. It is vital for separating similar memories."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hippocampus","Where does neurogenesis continue in the adult human brain?","The dentate gyrus (subgranular zone) and the subventricular zone of the lateral ventricles","New neurons are made and wired in through life. Stress reduces and antidepressants and exercise increase this in animal studies."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hippocampus","What is the main source of input to the hippocampus?","The entorhinal cortex (via the perforant path)","The entorhinal cortex funnels information from wide cortical areas. It is also the main target of hippocampal output."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hippocampus","An 83-year-old woman in the memory clinic has early Alzheimer's disease. Which brain region shows the earliest changes?","The entorhinal cortex (medial temporal lobe)","Tangles appear first in the transentorhinal and entorhinal cortex (Braak stages I to II). They then spread to the hippocampus and neocortex."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Amygdala","What are the main functions of the amygdala?","Fear conditioning, emotional memory and processing emotional faces","It rates how emotionally important an event is. It then boosts hippocampal storage of that event."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Amygdala","Can a person with bilateral amygdala damage still learn new neutral information?","Yes","The amygdala adds emotional weight to memories but is not needed for all consolidation. Neutral learning relies on the hippocampus."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Amygdala","What social change is seen in monkeys after amygdala lesions?","Loss of fear and impaired maternal behaviour","Without emotional tagging they approach threats and neglect infants. This mirrors parts of Kluver-Bucy syndrome."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Reward structures","Which structure is most central to reward processing?","The nucleus accumbens","It receives dopamine from the ventral tegmental area. Drugs of misuse all raise dopamine here."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Reward structures","Which part of the striatum contains the nucleus accumbens?","The ventral striatum","The dorsal striatum (caudate and putamen) serves motor and habit loops. The ventral striatum serves motivation and reward."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Reward structures","In which part of the brainstem is the ventral tegmental area?","The midbrain","Its dopamine neurons form the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways. It lies next to the substantia nigra."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hypothalamus and thalamus","Which hypothalamic nucleus acts as the satiety centre?","The ventromedial nucleus","Lesions cause overeating and obesity. Lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (feeding centre) cause loss of appetite."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hypothalamus and thalamus","A 40-year-old woman eats constantly and gains weight quickly after surgery for a craniopharyngioma. Damage to which hypothalamic nucleus is most likely?","The ventromedial nucleus","Loss of this satiety centre removes the signal to stop eating. Hypothalamic obesity is a known complication of tumours in this region."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Hypothalamus and thalamus","Which thalamic relay is part of the visual pathway?","The lateral geniculate body","Optic tract fibres synapse here, then the optic radiations go to the occipital cortex. The medial geniculate body relays hearing."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia structure","Which nuclei form the basal ganglia?","Caudate, putamen and globus pallidus (with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra as functional parts)","They form loops with the cortex and thalamus. These loops select and scale movement, habits and thoughts."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia structure","What makes up the lentiform (lenticular) nucleus?","The putamen and globus pallidus","Together they form a lens shape lateral to the internal capsule. The striatum is the caudate plus the putamen."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia structure","Which basal ganglia nucleus contains melanin pigment?","The substantia nigra (pars compacta)","Neuromelanin is a by-product of dopamine metabolism. Loss of these cells pales the nigra in Parkinson's disease."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia structure","Which neurotransmitter does the subthalamic nucleus use?","Glutamate","It is the only excitatory nucleus within the basal ganglia. It drives the inhibitory output of the internal globus pallidus."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia circuits","What does the direct basal ganglia pathway do to movement?","It facilitates movement","Striatal neurons with D1 receptors inhibit the internal pallidum. This releases the thalamus to excite the cortex."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia circuits","Which basal ganglia structures are implicated in OCD by imaging?","The caudate and lentiform nuclei, within the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical loop","PET shows altered activity in the orbitofrontal cortex and basal ganglia. Successful treatment tends to normalise it."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia circuits","Degeneration of which structure causes Huntington's disease?","The striatum, especially the caudate","Striatal GABAergic neurons are lost first. Caudate atrophy enlarges the frontal horns on imaging."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia circuits","A lesion of which nucleus causes hemiballismus?","The contralateral subthalamic nucleus","Loss of its excitatory drive releases the thalamus. This gives wild flinging movements of the opposite limbs."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Basal ganglia circuits","A 74-year-old woman with diabetes develops sudden violent flinging of her right arm. Which structure is most likely damaged?","The left subthalamic nucleus","Hemiballismus is usually from a small stroke in the opposite subthalamic nucleus. It often settles over weeks."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Cerebellum","Which neurons are found only in the cerebellum?","Purkinje cells","They are GABAergic and form the sole output of the cerebellar cortex. They inhibit the deep cerebellar nuclei."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Cerebellum","What kind of speech problem follows cerebellar damage?","Scanning (ataxic) dysarthria","Poor coordination of speech muscles breaks words into irregular, separate syllables. Language itself is normal."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Cerebellum","On which side of the body do signs of a cerebellar hemisphere lesion appear?","The same (ipsilateral) side","Cerebellar pathways cross twice, so the effects end up on the same side. Motor cortex lesions affect the opposite side."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Cerebellum","What is dysdiadochokinesia, and where does it localise?","Impaired rapid alternating movements; cerebellum","The cerebellum times opposing muscle groups. Damage makes fast switching clumsy and irregular."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Cerebellum","List four signs of a cerebellar lesion apart from dysarthria.","Ataxia, intention tremor, nystagmus and dysdiadochokinesia (also hypotonia, past pointing, pendular reflexes)","The cerebellum compares intended and actual movement. Without it movement overshoots and becomes uncoordinated."],
["Limbic system, basal ganglia and cerebellum","Cerebellum","A 55-year-old man with long-term heavy drinking has a broad-based unsteady gait but near normal arm coordination. Which part of the cerebellum is damaged?","The anterior superior vermis","The vermis controls trunk and leg coordination. Alcohol damages it first, sparing the hemispheres that control the arms."],
/* ---------- Neurons, glia and development ---------- */
["Neurons, glia and development","Neuron types","Which type of neuron has no axon?","Amacrine neurons","They connect locally through dendrites, for example in the retina. Golgi type I neurons have long axons; type II neurons have short local axons."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Neuron types","Where are electrical synapses found in the human nervous system?","Places needing fast synchrony, such as the retina, inferior olive and some interneuron networks","They use gap junctions made of connexins. Current passes directly and both ways with almost no delay."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","Which glial cells remove debris after neuronal death?","Microglia","They are the resident immune cells of the brain, related to macrophages. They also prune synapses in health."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","What is the developmental origin of microglia?","Mesoderm (yolk sac myeloid progenitors)","Unlike neurons and other glia, they do not come from ectoderm. This explains their likeness to macrophages."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","Which cells make myelin in the central nervous system?","Oligodendrocytes","One oligodendrocyte myelinates segments of many axons. They are the target in multiple sclerosis."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","Which glial cells make myelin in the peripheral nervous system?","Schwann cells","Each Schwann cell myelinates a single segment of one axon. They come from the neural crest."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","Name three functions of astrocytes.","Supporting the blood-brain barrier, buffering potassium, and taking up glutamate (also storing glycogen and feeding neurons)","Astrocyte end-feet wrap blood vessels. They do not make myelin; that is the role of oligodendrocytes."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","Which enzyme in astrocytes converts glutamate to glutamine?","Glutamine synthetase","Astrocytes take up synaptic glutamate and turn it into harmless glutamine. Neurons then convert glutamine back into glutamate."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Glia","Which cells line the ventricles?","Ependymal cells","They are ciliated and help move CSF. Specialised ependyma in the choroid plexus makes CSF."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Cell biology","What is the main role of an endosome?","Sorting and recycling membrane and receptors taken in by endocytosis","Material is either sent back to the membrane or on to lysosomes. This controls how many receptors sit on the cell surface."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Cell biology","Where in the cell are phospholipids made?","The smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth ER makes lipids and stores calcium. Rough ER, studded with ribosomes, makes proteins."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","Which embryonic division gives rise to the cerebral hemispheres?","The telencephalon","The forebrain (prosencephalon) splits into telencephalon and diencephalon. The telencephalon forms the cortex, basal ganglia and hippocampus."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","Which embryonic division gives rise to the thalamus and hypothalamus?","The diencephalon","It also gives the epithalamus (pineal), subthalamus and retina. It surrounds the third ventricle."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","Which embryonic division becomes the midbrain, and name two structures in it?","The mesencephalon; e.g. red nucleus and substantia nigra","Other midbrain structures are the ventral tegmental area, colliculi and the cerebral aqueduct. Cranial nerve nuclei III and IV lie here too."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","Where do neural crest cells come from?","The edges (neural folds) of the closing neural tube","They break away as the tube closes and migrate widely. They form peripheral neurons, Schwann cells, melanocytes and the adrenal medulla."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","By what point in pregnancy has the neural tube normally closed?","By the end of the fourth week after conception","Failure gives neural tube defects. Folic acid before conception lowers the risk, which matters with valproate."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","When is synapse formation at its peak?","In the first 2 years of life","Synapses are overproduced in infancy. Unused ones are then pruned, mostly through childhood and adolescence."],
["Neurons, glia and development","Embryology","Which structural variant of the septum pellucidum is reported more often in schizophrenia?","An enlarged cavum septum pellucidum","The two leaves of the septum fail to fuse after birth. It is seen as a marker of early neurodevelopmental disturbance."],
/* ---------- Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves ---------- */
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Ventricles and CSF","Which structure separates the two lateral ventricles?","The septum pellucidum","It is a thin membrane running from the corpus callosum down to the fornix. It forms the medial wall of each lateral ventricle."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Ventricles and CSF","What does the foramen of Monro connect?","Each lateral ventricle to the third ventricle","It is also called the interventricular foramen. Blockage here, for example by a colloid cyst, enlarges the lateral ventricles."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Ventricles and CSF","What connects the third and fourth ventricles?","The cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius)","It runs through the midbrain. Narrowing here is a classic cause of non-communicating hydrocephalus."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Ventricles and CSF","Through which ventricle does CSF leave to reach the subarachnoid space?","The fourth ventricle, via the foramina of Magendie and Luschka","Magendie is the single midline opening and Luschka the two lateral ones. Blockage here gives non-communicating hydrocephalus."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Ventricles and CSF","A 72-year-old man in the memory clinic has a shuffling, magnetic gait, urinary incontinence and slowed thinking. CT shows ventricles large for his age. What is the likely diagnosis?","Normal pressure hydrocephalus","It is a communicating hydrocephalus with the triad of gait, bladder and cognitive problems. A large-volume lumbar tap that improves gait supports shunting."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood supply","Which arteries supply the internal capsule?","Branches of the circle of Willis: lenticulostriate arteries (middle cerebral), recurrent artery of Heubner (anterior cerebral) and anterior choroidal artery (internal carotid)","No single artery supplies it all. Small vessel disease of the lenticulostriate arteries causes lacunar strokes here."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood supply","Which artery supplies most of the corpus callosum?","The anterior cerebral artery","Its pericallosal branch runs along the top of the callosum. The posterior cerebral artery supplies the splenium."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood supply","Which artery is the main supply of the hippocampus?","The posterior cerebral artery","It also supplies the occipital lobe and inferomedial temporal lobe. Bilateral occlusion can cause dense amnesia."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood supply","What does a posterior cerebral artery occlusion classically cause?","A contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing","The macula often keeps a dual supply from the middle cerebral artery. Medial temporal involvement may add memory loss."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood supply","A 68-year-old woman suddenly develops weakness of her right leg, apathy and urinary incontinence. Which artery is most likely blocked?","The left anterior cerebral artery","It supplies the medial frontal lobe, including the leg area of motor cortex. Medial frontal damage adds abulia and incontinence."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood-brain barrier","What kind of molecules cross the blood-brain barrier most easily?","Small lipid-soluble (lipophilic) molecules","They dissolve through the endothelial cell membranes. Water-soluble molecules need specific carriers because of the tight junctions."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Blood-brain barrier","Name three circumventricular organs that lack a full blood-brain barrier.","Area postrema, posterior pituitary and median eminence (also the pineal, subfornical organ and OVLT)","These areas must sense or release substances in the blood. Leaky capillaries allow this."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Spinal tracts","What does the anterior spinothalamic tract carry?","Crude touch and pressure","It crosses close to its level of entry and runs up the opposite side. Fine touch travels in the dorsal columns instead."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Spinal tracts","What does the lateral spinothalamic tract carry?","Pain and temperature","Fibres cross within one or two segments of entry. So a cord lesion causes loss of pain sense on the opposite side below it."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Spinal tracts","What do the spinocerebellar tracts carry?","Unconscious proprioception (with some touch and pressure) to the cerebellum","They tell the cerebellum about limb position and movement. This lets it coordinate motion without awareness."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Spinal tracts","What does the lateral corticospinal tract carry, and where does it cross?","Voluntary motor commands; it crosses at the pyramids of the medulla","Most fibres decussate in the lower medulla. So lesions above this cause weakness on the opposite side."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","White matter bundles","Which white matter tract links the orbitofrontal cortex with the anterior temporal lobe and amygdala?","The uncinate fasciculus","This hook-shaped tract joins frontal control with limbic emotion areas. Changes in it are reported in psychopathy and frontotemporal dementia."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","White matter bundles","Which long association bundle links the frontal lobe with the parietal, temporal and occipital lobes?","The superior longitudinal fasciculus","It runs front to back lateral to the centrum semiovale. The arcuate fasciculus is closely related to it."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","White matter bundles","Which white matter tract is most linked to visual object recognition?","The inferior longitudinal fasciculus","It joins the occipital and temporal lobes. Damage is linked to visual agnosia and prosopagnosia."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","White matter bundles","Which two structures form the standard reference line for stereotactic brain surgery?","The anterior and posterior commissures (the AC-PC line)","They are easy to see on MRI and stable between people. Deep brain stimulation targets are set relative to them."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Visual pathway","A lesion at which site causes a bitemporal hemianopia?","The optic chiasm","Crossing nasal retinal fibres carry the temporal fields. A pituitary tumour pressing from below is the usual cause."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Motor and sensory nerves","Which cranial nerves are purely sensory?","I (olfactory), II (optic) and VIII (vestibulocochlear)","They carry only special senses. They have no motor fibres to muscles."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Motor and sensory nerves","Which cranial nerves are purely motor?","III, IV, VI, XI and XII","III also carries parasympathetic fibres to the pupil, but none of these carry general sensation."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Motor and sensory nerves","Which single muscle does the abducens nerve supply?","The lateral rectus","It turns the eye outward. The abducens nerve has no other function."],
["Ventricles, vessels, tracts and nerves","Eye movement palsies","A 55-year-old man has double vision on looking left. His left eye will not move outward. Which nerve is affected?","The left abducens nerve (VI)","The unopposed medial rectus pulls the eye inward. There is no ptosis or pupil change, unlike a third nerve palsy."],
/* ---------- Membranes, action potentials and synapses ---------- */
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Resting potential","What is the typical resting membrane potential of a neuron?","About -70 mV","The inside is negative relative to the outside. This is close to the potassium equilibrium potential."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Resting potential","Which ion is most concentrated inside a neuron at rest?","Potassium","The sodium-potassium pump moves potassium in. Its outward leak through open channels makes the inside negative."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Resting potential","Which ion is most concentrated outside a neuron at rest?","Sodium (with chloride also high outside)","The sodium-potassium pump keeps intracellular sodium low. This steep gradient powers the action potential."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Resting potential","What does the sodium-potassium pump move per cycle?","3 sodium ions out and 2 potassium ions in, using one ATP","Unequal charge movement makes it slightly electrogenic. Its main role is keeping the gradients that set the resting potential."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Resting potential","Which ion often distributes passively across the neuronal membrane at rest?","Chloride","In many mature neurons chloride is not actively pumped much, so it settles near the resting potential. Its flow mainly shapes inhibition."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Action potential","What causes the rising (depolarising) phase of the action potential?","Influx of sodium through voltage-gated sodium channels","Once threshold (about -55 mV) is reached, channels open in a positive feedback loop. The membrane swings towards the sodium equilibrium potential."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Action potential","What causes repolarisation of the action potential?","Outflow of potassium through voltage-gated potassium channels","Sodium channels also inactivate. Potassium leaving restores the negative interior."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Action potential","What causes the absolute refractory period?","Inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels","No stimulus can trigger another spike until the channels reset. This makes impulses travel one way only."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Action potential","How does myelin speed up nerve conduction?","It allows saltatory conduction, with the impulse jumping between nodes of Ranvier","Myelin lowers capacitance and stops current leaking. Sodium channels cluster at the nodes, where the spike is renewed."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Synaptic transmission","What triggers neurotransmitter release at the synapse?","Calcium entry through voltage-gated calcium channels in the presynaptic terminal","Calcium binds sensors such as synaptotagmin. SNARE proteins then fuse vesicles with the membrane."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Synaptic transmission","What ion movement usually produces a fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential?","Chloride entry (through GABA-A or glycine receptors)","The negative charge moves the membrane away from threshold. Potassium efflux via GABA-B receptors gives slower inhibition."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Brain stimulation","Which brain region is targeted by high-frequency rTMS to treat depression?","The left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex","This area is underactive in depression. Repeated magnetic pulses induce currents that raise its activity."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Brain stimulation","What happens to beta-adrenergic receptors after a course of ECT?","They are down-regulated","Antidepressant drugs cause the same change. It is one of the shared receptor effects linked to recovery."],
["Membranes, action potentials and synapses","Brain stimulation","A 70-year-old man having ECT for psychotic depression has shorter seizures at the same dose as the course goes on. What has happened to his seizure threshold?","It has risen","The brain adapts to repeated seizures. Stimulus dose may need to be increased later in the course."],
/* ---------- EEG ---------- */
["EEG","Wave frequencies","What is the frequency of delta waves?","Less than 4 Hz","Delta is normal in deep sleep and infancy. In an awake adult it points to brain dysfunction."],
["EEG","Wave frequencies","What is the frequency of theta waves?","4 to under 8 Hz","Theta is seen in drowsiness, light sleep and children. Some theta in awake adults is normal, but excess suggests slowing."],
["EEG","Wave frequencies","What is the frequency of alpha waves?","8 to 13 Hz","Alpha is the normal resting rhythm of the awake adult. It is best seen at the back of the head."],
["EEG","Wave frequencies","What is the frequency of beta waves?","More than 13 Hz","Beta is seen frontally in alert people. It increases with benzodiazepines and barbiturates."],
["EEG","Wave frequencies","What is the mu rhythm?","An arch-shaped rhythm of about 7 to 11 Hz over the motor cortex, blocked by movement","It is in the alpha range but central, not occipital. Moving or even thinking of moving the opposite hand suppresses it."],
["EEG","Normal EEG","When is the alpha rhythm best seen?","Over the occipital region when awake, relaxed and with eyes closed","It is blocked when the eyes open or the person concentrates. This is called alpha blocking."],
["EEG","Normal EEG","Is continuous delta activity normal in an awake adult?","No, it is almost always abnormal","Delta is normal only in deep sleep or young children. Awake delta suggests a lesion, encephalopathy or delirium."],
["EEG","Normal EEG","A healthy 3-month-old boy has an awake EEG. Which waves dominate the recording?","Delta and theta","The background is slow in infancy. Faster rhythms appear as the brain matures."],
["EEG","Epilepsy","Roughly what proportion of people with epilepsy have a normal routine EEG between seizures?","About 30 to 50%","A single interictal EEG often misses discharges. Sleep deprivation or repeat recording raises the yield, and a normal EEG never rules out epilepsy."],
["EEG","Epilepsy","An 8-year-old girl referred to CAMHS for 'daydreaming' has brief staring spells many times a day. Over-breathing in clinic triggers one. What would the EEG show?","Generalised 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges","These are absence seizures, not inattention. They usually respond to ethosuximide or valproate."],
["EEG","Epilepsy","What EEG pattern is typical of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy?","Generalised polyspike-and-wave discharges, often with a photoparoxysmal response","Morning jerks are the clue. Flashing lights during the EEG can provoke the discharges."],
["EEG","Epilepsy","What EEG finding is typical of focal (partial) seizures?","Localised spikes or sharp waves over the seizure focus","The discharge stays in one area unless it spreads. Its site suggests where the seizure starts."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","A 64-year-old woman has a rapidly progressive dementia over 3 months with myoclonic jerks. What EEG pattern would support sporadic CJD?","Periodic generalised sharp-wave complexes at about 1 to 2 Hz","They appear as the disease progresses. They are usually absent in variant CJD."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","A 14-year-old boy has declining school work, then myoclonic jerks. The EEG shows high-voltage complexes repeating every 4 to 15 seconds. What is the likely diagnosis?","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","It is a late complication of measles infection. The periodic complexes match the myoclonic jerks."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","A 52-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis, seen by the A&E liaison team, is confused with a flapping tremor. Which EEG waves are typical?","Triphasic waves","They are seen in metabolic encephalopathies, especially liver failure. Generalised slowing is also present."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","What happens to the EEG in Huntington's disease?","It becomes low voltage and flattened","Cortical and striatal neuron loss reduces the electrical signal. Alpha is lost early."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","What is the typical EEG change in Alzheimer's disease?","Reduced alpha with increased theta and later delta","The background slows as the disease advances. Early on the EEG may be normal."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","A 79-year-old woman on an acute medical ward is confused and drowsy with a urinary infection. What would her EEG most likely show?","Generalised slowing, with reduced alpha and increased theta and delta","Slowing tracks the severity of delirium. Delirium tremens is the exception, where fast activity is seen."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","Is there a characteristic EEG pattern in schizophrenia?","No","Non-specific changes may occur, but nothing is diagnostic. An EEG is used mainly to exclude organic causes."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","Which EEG abnormality was classically reported in people with psychopathy?","Immature, slow waves over the posterior temporal regions","This finding suggested delayed cortical maturation. It is not specific or diagnostic."],
["EEG","Neuropsychiatric conditions","By roughly what age are characteristic EEG changes of Angelman syndrome usually evident?","By about 2 years","The EEG shows high-amplitude rhythmic slow activity, often with notched spikes. It can help suggest the diagnosis early."],
["EEG","Drug effects","A 38-year-old woman taking regular diazepam has a routine EEG. What drug-related change is most likely?","Increased beta activity with reduced alpha","This frontal fast activity is a common drug artefact. It does not mean seizure activity."],
["EEG","Drug effects","What is the main EEG effect of barbiturates?","Increased beta activity with reduced alpha","Like benzodiazepines, they act on GABA-A receptors. At high doses the EEG slows and may flatten."],
["EEG","Drug effects","What is the general EEG effect of antipsychotics?","Increased slow-wave (theta and delta) activity","Changes are greater with higher doses. Clozapine causes the most EEG changes."],
["EEG","Drug effects","What is the acute EEG effect of alcohol?","Increased alpha and theta activity","Slowing reflects sedation. In withdrawal and delirium tremens, fast activity appears instead."],
["EEG","Drug effects","What is the acute EEG effect of opioids?","Increased theta and delta (slowing)","Opioid sedation slows the background. Naloxone reverses this."],
["EEG","Drug effects","What is the EEG effect of cannabis?","Increased alpha activity","Users often show more frontal alpha. The change is non-specific."],
/* ---------- Sleep and circadian rhythms ---------- */
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","What EEG features mark stage N2 sleep?","Sleep spindles and K-complexes","Spindles are 12 to 14 Hz bursts and K-complexes are large biphasic waves. N2 takes up about half of adult sleep."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","Which stages make up slow-wave sleep?","N3 (old stages 3 and 4 of non-REM sleep)","It is defined by high-amplitude delta waves. It is the deepest sleep and hardest to wake from."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","What does the EEG look like in REM sleep?","Low-voltage, mixed-frequency activity resembling wakefulness, with sawtooth waves","This is why REM is called paradoxical sleep. The body is paralysed while the brain is active."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","Is muscle tone in REM sleep similar to wakefulness?","No, there is atonia of most skeletal muscles","Brainstem circuits inhibit motor neurons. The diaphragm and eye muscles are spared."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","From which sleep stage does waking give the best dream recall?","REM sleep","REM dreams are vivid and story-like. Reports from non-REM awakenings are shorter and more thought-like."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","What proportion of adult sleep is REM?","About 20 to 25%","REM periods lengthen through the night. N2 is about half and N3 about 15 to 20%."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","What is a normal sleep onset latency in a healthy adult?","About 10 to 20 minutes","Much shorter suggests sleep deprivation or a sleep disorder. On the MSLT, a mean under 8 minutes is pathological."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","A 42-year-old healthy man sleeps 10 pm to 5:30 am and has a sleep study. About how long after sleep onset will his first REM period begin?","About 90 minutes","This is the normal REM latency. A much shorter latency is seen in depression and narcolepsy."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep stages","A 48-year-old woman with severe depression has a sleep study. What change in REM latency is expected?","It is shortened","Other changes are reduced slow-wave sleep, more REM early in the night and early waking. Most antidepressants suppress REM."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep across life","Which sleep stage dominates in newborns?","REM (active) sleep, about half of total sleep","REM may help drive brain development. Its share falls to adult levels by adolescence."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep across life","How does sleep change in older adults?","Less slow-wave sleep, more awakenings and an earlier sleep phase","Total night sleep falls and naps rise. Daytime napping is a normal pattern, not always disease."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Circadian rhythms","Which nucleus is the master circadian clock?","The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus","It sits just above the optic chiasm. Light reaches it via the retinohypothalamic tract."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Circadian rhythms","Where is melatonin made, and what drives its release?","The pineal gland; darkness","The suprachiasmatic nucleus drives release at night via a sympathetic pathway. Light suppresses it."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep regulation","Which neurons are lost in narcolepsy with cataplexy?","Orexin (hypocretin) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus","Orexin stabilises wakefulness and blocks REM intrusion. CSF orexin is low or absent."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep regulation","A 22-year-old woman tells her GP she falls asleep in lectures and her knees buckle when she laughs. What is the likely cause?","Narcolepsy type 1 with cataplexy, due to loss of orexin neurons","Cataplexy is REM atonia intruding into wakefulness. Sleep studies show sleep-onset REM periods."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep regulation","Which substance builds up during wakefulness and drives sleep pressure?","Adenosine","It accumulates as the brain uses energy. Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, which is how it keeps people awake."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep regulation","Which neurons switch REM sleep on?","Cholinergic neurons of the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei","Noradrenergic and serotonergic neurons fall silent in REM. Their silence lets the cholinergic 'REM-on' cells fire."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Drugs and sleep","How do benzodiazepines change sleep architecture?","More stage N2 (spindles), less slow-wave sleep and some REM suppression","They shorten sleep latency and lengthen total sleep. The sleep gained is light, not restorative deep sleep."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Drugs and sleep","A 19-year-old man stops taking amphetamines after a binge. He sleeps for long periods with very vivid dreams. What explains the dreams?","REM rebound","Stimulants suppress REM sleep. When they stop, REM returns in excess."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Drugs and sleep","Stopping which kind of prescribed stimulant for ADHD can cause REM rebound?","Methylphenidate (and amphetamines)","These drugs suppress REM through extra dopamine and noradrenaline. REM rebounds when they are withdrawn."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep disorders","What is the core feature of REM sleep behaviour disorder?","Loss of normal REM atonia, so people act out their dreams","It often comes years before Parkinson's disease or Lewy body dementia. It reflects early alpha-synuclein brainstem damage."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep disorders","A 66-year-old man's wife says he punches and shouts in his sleep, often near morning, and he recalls vivid dreams. What is the likely diagnosis?","REM sleep behaviour disorder","Episodes occur in REM, which is richer later in the night. It is a strong early marker of synucleinopathy."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep disorders","From which sleep stage do night terrors and sleepwalking arise?","Slow-wave sleep (N3)","They occur in the first third of the night, with little memory after. Nightmares arise from REM later in the night and are recalled."],
["Sleep and circadian rhythms","Sleep disorders","What are the core features of Kleine-Levin syndrome?","Recurrent episodes of hypersomnia with hyperphagia, hypersexuality and cognitive or mood change","It mainly affects adolescent males. Between episodes the person is normal."],
/* ---------- Neuroendocrinology ---------- */
["Neuroendocrinology","Pituitary","Which hormones are released from the posterior pituitary?","Oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH)","They are made in the hypothalamus and carried down axons. The posterior pituitary stores and releases them."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Pituitary","Name a substance that acts both as a hormone and as a CNS neurotransmitter.","Vasopressin (also oxytocin)","Released into blood, it controls water balance. Released in the brain, it affects social behaviour and memory."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Pituitary","What is the main control on prolactin release?","Tonic inhibition by dopamine from the tuberoinfundibular pathway","Dopamine acts on D2 receptors on pituitary lactotrophs. Blocking these receptors raises prolactin."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Pituitary","Besides TSH, which hormone does TRH stimulate?","Prolactin","This is why primary hypothyroidism, with high TRH, can raise prolactin. A blunted TSH response to TRH is seen in some depressed people."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Pituitary","A 29-year-old woman with bipolar disorder on an antipsychotic has missed her periods for 3 months. What is the first test?","A pregnancy test","Pregnancy is the commonest cause of amenorrhoea and changes drug choices. Then check prolactin."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","Where is CRH produced?","The paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus","CRH drives ACTH release from the anterior pituitary. ACTH then drives cortisol from the adrenal cortex."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","When does cortisol normally peak?","In the early morning, around 6 to 8 am","It rises before waking and falls to a low around midnight. Samples must be timed for this reason."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","A healthy 40-year-old man takes 1 mg dexamethasone at 11 pm. What happens to his ACTH and cortisol the next morning?","Both are suppressed","Dexamethasone acts on pituitary glucocorticoid receptors as negative feedback. Failure to suppress suggests HPA overactivity."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","Roughly what proportion of people with severe (melancholic) depression fail to suppress cortisol on the dexamethasone test?","About half","HPA axis overactivity is common in severe depression. The test is not specific, so it is not used for diagnosis."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","What happens to CRH in depression?","It is raised (for example in CSF)","Excess CRH drives high ACTH and cortisol. It may also cause poor sleep, appetite loss and anxiety."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","A 33-year-old male army veteran with chronic PTSD has his HPA axis tested. What cortisol pattern is typical?","Low-normal cortisol with exaggerated suppression to dexamethasone","PTSD shows increased negative feedback sensitivity. Depression tends to show high cortisol and poor suppression."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","What type of receptor is the glucocorticoid receptor?","An intracellular (nuclear) receptor that acts as a ligand-dependent transcription factor","Cortisol enters the cell and binds it in the cytoplasm. The complex moves to the nucleus and changes gene expression."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Adrenal and thyroid","A 45-year-old man in A&E has apathy, weight loss, low sodium, high potassium and darker skin. What is the likely diagnosis?","Addison's disease (primary adrenal insufficiency)","Low aldosterone causes salt loss and potassium retention. High ACTH causes the pigmentation; confirm with a short Synacthen test."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Adrenal and thyroid","A 47-year-old woman with untreated hypothyroidism has low mood. Is a fast heart rate in keeping with her diagnosis?","No, hypothyroidism causes bradycardia","Low thyroid hormone slows metabolism and heart rate. Tachycardia points to thyroid excess or another cause."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Growth and appetite","During which sleep stage does growth hormone surge?","Slow-wave sleep","The biggest pulse comes in the first slow-wave episode of the night. Loss of deep sleep reduces it."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Growth and appetite","What growth hormone finding is seen in depression after a clonidine challenge?","A blunted growth hormone response","Clonidine stimulates GH via alpha-2 receptors. A blunted rise suggests altered noradrenergic function."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Growth and appetite","Which hypothalamic peptide strongly increases food intake?","Neuropeptide Y","It is released from the arcuate nucleus. Leptin inhibits it and ghrelin stimulates it."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Growth and appetite","Which stomach hormone stimulates hunger?","Ghrelin","Levels rise before meals and fall after eating. It acts on the hypothalamus to drive appetite."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Growth and appetite","Which hormone acts as the body's long-term signal of stored energy?","Leptin","It is made by fat cells in proportion to fat mass. It acts on the hypothalamus to reduce appetite."],
/* ---------- Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown ---------- */
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Classes","Which neurotransmitters are catecholamines?","Dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline","All have a catechol ring and are made from tyrosine. Serotonin is an indoleamine instead."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Classes","Name the three main amino acid neurotransmitters.","Glutamate, GABA and glycine","Glutamate excites. GABA and glycine inhibit."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Classes","Name three neuropeptide transmitters.","For example endorphins, cholecystokinin and substance P (also neuropeptide Y, neurotensin, CRH)","They are chains of amino acids made in the cell body. They usually act slowly as modulators."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","What is the amino acid precursor of dopamine and noradrenaline?","Tyrosine","Tyrosine comes from the diet or from phenylalanine. It is converted to L-DOPA, then dopamine, then noradrenaline."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","What is the rate-limiting enzyme in dopamine synthesis?","Tyrosine hydroxylase","It turns tyrosine into L-DOPA. Giving L-DOPA in Parkinson's disease bypasses this step."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","Which enzyme converts L-DOPA to dopamine?","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase)","It also turns 5-HTP into serotonin and needs vitamin B6. Carbidopa blocks it outside the brain."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","What is the amino acid precursor of serotonin?","L-tryptophan","It is an essential amino acid from the diet. Acute tryptophan depletion can bring back low mood in recovered depressed people."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","What is the rate-limiting enzyme in serotonin synthesis?","Tryptophan hydroxylase","It turns tryptophan into 5-HTP. The brain form is coded by the TPH2 gene."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","What is the precursor of histamine?","Histidine","Histidine decarboxylase removes a carboxyl group. Brain histamine neurons lie in the tuberomammillary nucleus."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","Which enzyme makes acetylcholine?","Choline acetyltransferase","It joins choline and acetyl-CoA. Choline uptake into the terminal is the rate-limiting step."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Synthesis","What is the precursor of GABA, and which enzyme converts it?","Glutamate, via glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD)","GAD needs vitamin B6. So the main inhibitory transmitter is made from the main excitatory one."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Breakdown","Which enzyme breaks down GABA?","GABA transaminase","It makes succinic semialdehyde, which is turned into succinic acid for the Krebs cycle. Vigabatrin works by blocking this enzyme."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Breakdown","What is the main end product of GABA breakdown?","Succinic acid (succinate)","GABA transaminase makes succinic semialdehyde. This is then oxidised to succinate."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Breakdown","Which enzyme breaks down acetylcholine, and where?","Acetylcholinesterase, in the synaptic cleft on the postsynaptic side","It splits acetylcholine into choline and acetate. The choline is taken back up and reused."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Breakdown","Which form of monoamine oxidase mainly breaks down serotonin and noradrenaline?","MAO-A","It also metabolises dietary tyramine in the gut. Blocking it causes the tyramine 'cheese reaction'."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Breakdown","Which form of monoamine oxidase mainly breaks down dopamine in the human brain?","MAO-B (with a share from MAO-A)","MAO-B is mainly in glia. Selegiline and rasagiline block it to boost dopamine in Parkinson's disease."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Metabolites","What is the main metabolite of dopamine?","Homovanillic acid (HVA)","It is made by MAO and COMT acting together. CSF HVA reflects central dopamine turnover."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Metabolites","What is the main metabolite of noradrenaline in the brain?","MHPG (3-methoxy-4-hydroxyphenylglycol)","In the periphery the main metabolite is vanillylmandelic acid (VMA)."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Metabolites","What is the main peripheral metabolite of noradrenaline?","Vanillylmandelic acid (VMA)","Urine VMA is raised in phaeochromocytoma. In the brain, MHPG is the main product."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Metabolites","What is the main metabolite of serotonin?","5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA)","MAO-A converts serotonin to it. CSF levels reflect central serotonin turnover."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Metabolites","Low CSF 5-HIAA has been linked with which behaviours?","Impulsive aggression and violent suicide attempts","Low serotonin turnover may weaken impulse control. This was one of the first biological markers of suicide risk."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Enzymes in disease","Which enzyme activity is reduced in the cortex in Alzheimer's disease?","Choline acetyltransferase (with acetylcholinesterase also reduced)","Cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert are lost. This is the basis for cholinesterase inhibitor treatment."],
["Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown","Enzymes in disease","Which enzyme and transmitter are reduced in the striatum in Huntington's disease?","Glutamic acid decarboxylase and GABA","GABAergic medium spiny neurons die early. Less GABA output releases movement, causing chorea."],
/* ---------- Receptors and pathways ---------- */
["Receptors and pathways","Receptor types","Name four ionotropic (ligand-gated ion channel) receptors.","Nicotinic, GABA-A, glycine, NMDA/AMPA (and 5-HT3)","The receptor is itself the channel, so they act in milliseconds. Metabotropic receptors act through G proteins and are slower."],
["Receptors and pathways","Receptor types","Which serotonin receptor is a ligand-gated ion channel?","5-HT3","It is a cation channel; all other 5-HT receptors are G protein-coupled. Ondansetron blocks it to prevent vomiting."],
["Receptors and pathways","Receptor types","What type of receptor is GABA-A?","An ionotropic, ligand-gated chloride channel","It has five subunits around a pore. Benzodiazepines, barbiturates and alcohol enhance it."],
["Receptors and pathways","Receptor types","Which adrenergic receptors increase adenylate cyclase activity?","Beta-adrenergic receptors","They couple to Gs, raising cAMP. Alpha-2 receptors couple to Gi and lower cAMP; alpha-1 receptors use Gq."],
["Receptors and pathways","Dopamine","Where do presynaptic D2 autoreceptors act?","On dopamine neuron terminals and cell bodies, inhibiting dopamine release","They give negative feedback. Low-dose amisulpride may block them and raise dopamine release."],
["Receptors and pathways","Dopamine","Where are D1 and D2 receptors most densely found?","The striatum (caudate and putamen)","This is where nigrostriatal dopamine fibres end. It explains why D2 blockade causes motor side effects."],
["Receptors and pathways","Dopamine","Which dopamine pathway controls prolactin release?","The tuberoinfundibular pathway","It runs from the arcuate nucleus to the pituitary stalk. Dopamine here inhibits prolactin."],
["Receptors and pathways","Dopamine","A 44-year-old woman on a depot antipsychotic has a milky nipple discharge and raised prolactin. Blockade of which pathway explains this?","The tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathway","D2 blockade removes dopamine's brake on prolactin. Switching to a prolactin-sparing drug such as aripiprazole may help."],
["Receptors and pathways","Dopamine","A 30-year-old man on haloperidol on an acute ward develops a tremor, rigidity and a shuffling gait. Blockade of which pathway is responsible?","The nigrostriatal pathway","It runs from the substantia nigra to the striatum. D2 blockade here mimics Parkinson's disease."],
["Receptors and pathways","Dopamine","Which dopamine pathway is linked to positive psychotic symptoms?","The mesolimbic pathway","It runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens. Excess dopamine here is thought to cause delusions and hallucinations."],
["Receptors and pathways","Glutamate","What two molecules must bind the NMDA receptor for it to open?","Glutamate plus a co-agonist, glycine or D-serine","The cell must also be depolarised to remove the magnesium block. This makes it a detector of coinciding activity."],
["Receptors and pathways","Glutamate","Which receptor is central to long-term potentiation?","The NMDA receptor","Its opening lets calcium in, which triggers more AMPA receptors at the synapse. This strengthening is a basis of learning and memory."],
["Receptors and pathways","Glutamate","A 25-year-old man brought to a health-based place of safety under Section 136 is psychotic after taking ketamine. How does ketamine act at the NMDA receptor?","It is a non-competitive antagonist that blocks the open channel","It is not an agonist. Phencyclidine acts the same way, and both can mimic schizophrenia."],
["Receptors and pathways","Glutamate","NMDA receptors on which cells are thought to be most affected in the glutamate model of schizophrenia?","Inhibitory GABA interneurons","Their hypofunction weakens inhibition of pyramidal cells. Cortical and dopamine overactivity follow."],
["Receptors and pathways","Glutamate","What is the main fast excitatory neurotransmitter in the brain?","Glutamate","It acts at AMPA, kainate and NMDA receptors. Excess release causes excitotoxic cell death."],
["Receptors and pathways","GABA and glycine","Is GABA arranged in long discrete pathways?","No, it is spread widely, mainly in local interneurons","GABA is the main inhibitory transmitter throughout the brain. Exceptions with long GABA projections include Purkinje and striatal neurons."],
["Receptors and pathways","GABA and glycine","Where is glycine the main inhibitory transmitter?","The spinal cord and brainstem","It opens chloride channels. Strychnine blocks its receptor, causing convulsions."],
["Receptors and pathways","Monoamine nuclei","Where are most noradrenergic cell bodies in the brain?","The locus coeruleus in the pons","From here a few neurons project widely to the cortex. They drive arousal and alertness."],
["Receptors and pathways","Monoamine nuclei","Where are most serotonergic cell bodies in the brain?","The raphe nuclei (dorsal and median) of the brainstem","They project widely to the cortex, limbic system and spinal cord. Most of the body's serotonin is in the gut, outside the brain."],
["Receptors and pathways","Monoamine nuclei","Which basal forebrain nucleus is the main source of cortical acetylcholine?","The nucleus basalis of Meynert","Its neurons project widely to the cortex. They degenerate early in Alzheimer's disease."],
["Receptors and pathways","Acetylcholine and nicotine","Which neurotransmitter does nicotine release in the reward pathway?","Dopamine","Nicotine acts on alpha4-beta2 nicotinic receptors on VTA neurons. This raises dopamine in the nucleus accumbens."],
["Receptors and pathways","Acetylcholine and nicotine","A 35-year-old man on a CMHT caseload smokes 30 cigarettes a day. Which receptors does the smoke directly stimulate?","Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors","These ion channels open when nicotine binds. Smoking also induces CYP1A2, which matters for clozapine and olanzapine levels."],
["Receptors and pathways","Opioids and cannabinoids","Which opioid receptor mainly mediates supraspinal analgesia and euphoria?","The mu receptor","Morphine and beta-endorphin act here. It also causes respiratory depression and miosis."],
["Receptors and pathways","Opioids and cannabinoids","Which receptors do anandamide and THC act on?","Cannabinoid receptors (CB1 in the brain, CB2 mainly in immune cells)","CB1 is one of the most common G protein-coupled receptors in the brain. Its activation reduces transmitter release."],
["Receptors and pathways","Serotonin receptors","Which serotonin receptor is linked to circadian rhythm?","5-HT7","It is found in the suprachiasmatic nucleus. Some antidepressants and antipsychotics block it."],
["Receptors and pathways","Serotonin receptors","Agonism at which serotonin receptor is linked to antidepressant and anxiolytic effects?","5-HT1A","Buspirone is a partial agonist here. Somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors desensitise over weeks of SSRI use."],
["Receptors and pathways","Serotonin receptors","Which serotonin receptor is linked to anxiety and reduced appetite when stimulated?","5-HT2C","Blocking it increases appetite and weight, as with olanzapine. Agonists such as mCPP provoke anxiety."],
["Receptors and pathways","Serotonin receptors","Which serotonin receptors do triptans act on to relieve migraine?","5-HT1B and 5-HT1D","Agonism narrows cranial vessels and blocks pain peptide release. Triptans plus serotonergic drugs carry a small serotonin syndrome risk."],
["Receptors and pathways","Serotonin receptors","A 21-year-old woman is seen in A&E with vivid visual distortions after eating magic mushrooms. Which receptor mainly causes these effects?","5-HT2A (agonism)","Magic mushrooms contain psilocybin, which is converted to psilocin. Its effects closely resemble LSD."],
["Receptors and pathways","Drugs of misuse","A 30-year-old man under the CMHT chews khat leaves daily and has become suspicious and restless. Which drug class do khat's effects resemble?","Amphetamines","Its active ingredient, cathinone, releases dopamine and noradrenaline. Users show alertness, euphoria and, with heavy use, psychosis."],
["Receptors and pathways","Transmitters and functions","Which transmitter is most linked to feeding, mood and sleep?","Serotonin","Many 5-HT receptor subtypes control appetite and sleep. This is why SSRIs change both."],
["Receptors and pathways","Transmitters and functions","Which neurotransmitter is mainly linked to anxiety disorders?","GABA","Low GABA tone is linked to anxiety. Benzodiazepines enhance GABA-A to relieve it."],
["Receptors and pathways","Transmitters and functions","Which neurotransmitter is mainly abnormal in Alzheimer's disease?","Acetylcholine","Loss of cholinergic neurons leads to poor attention and memory. Dopamine is the key transmitter in Parkinson's disease, not Alzheimer's."]
]);
